            <ArticleTitle>[Symptomatic bilateral soft tissue tumor of the breast wall].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>388-91</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00117-011-2174-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 54-year-old woman presented with progressive swelling of the right scapula as well as motion-dependent pain. The medical history revealed a polyglandular autoimmune syndrome and lumbar disc degeneration. The magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan showed a bilateral well-defined muscle isointense space-occupying lesion situated between the scapula, rib cage and thoracic muscles with homogeneous contrast enhancement. The constellation is indicative of a elastofibroma dorsi, a rare mesenchymal tumor often appearing bilaterally.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
